# Requires: library
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  DESIGN DRAFT — Codex proposal for a consistent `cascade<T>`
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Important note
# ─────────────────
# This file is EXPLICITLY not to be read as an existing AIML specification.
# It describes my idea of a consistent `cascade<T>` concept,
# if we want to model nested cascades as a real language type.
#
#
# The goal is not to map the current card editor 1:1, but to cut the
# core idea cleanly:
#
#   `cascade<T>` is a tagged variant value with a node-specific payload.
#
# Everything else — prefab bindings, widgets, list buttons, bytecode encoder,
# inspector tools — should only build on this invariant AFTERWARDS.
#
# 1) Core idea
# 1) Kernidee
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Many gameplay structures are neither flat enums nor flat
# models. They are: "choice of a node" + "fields that only exist for this
# node".
#
# Examples:
#   - ValueType.fixed(value)
#   - ValueType.equal_to_attribute(attribute)
#   - DurationType.turns(value_type)
#   - EffectCode.modify_stat(owner, attribute, value, duration)
#
# Conceptually that is an algebraic data type / tagged union / sum type.
# In AIML I call it `cascade<T>` here.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 2) Semantic invariants
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# A value of type `cascade<X>` always has exactly this structure:
#
#   - one active node tag
#   - exactly the payload fields of this active node
#   - no payload of other nodes
#
# The most important invariants follow from that:
#
#   I1. `cascade<X>` is never "several nodes at the same time".
#   I2. A node change discards the old node payload completely.
#   I3. The payload is typed; a field knows its real type.
#   I4. Recursion is allowed, but only as a finite tree value.
#   I5. Serialisation uses the node ID + payload in declared order.
#
# Open question that a real specification MUST decide explicitly:
#
#   Is there an empty state?
#
# My proposal for consistency:
#   No, not implicitly.
#
# If "not set yet" is needed, then explicitly via:
#   - `optional<cascade<X>>`, or
#   - a real node like `unset` / `none`
#
# That way `cascade<X>` itself stays total and unambiguous.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 3) Language form
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# `cascade` is a top-level type builder, analogous to `model` or `enum`.
# Every node has:
#   - a stable ID
#   - a name
#   - optionally a label
#   - zero or more typed fields
#
# Example syntax:

- cascade:
    name: ValueType
    nodes:
      - fixed:
          id: 1
          label: "Fixed"
          fields:
            - [value, i32]
      - equal_to_mana_cost:
          id: 2
          label: "Equal to Mana Cost"
      - equal_to_attribute:
          id: 3
          label: "Equal to Attribute"
          fields:
            - [attribute, AttributeType]
      - equal_to_cards_on_deck:
          id: 4
          label: "Equal to Cards on Deck"
          fields:
            - [deck, DeckType]
            - [owner, OwnerType]
      - equal_to_cards_in_zone:
          id: 5
          label: "Equal to Cards in Zone"
          fields:
            - [zone, GameZone]
            - [owner, OwnerType]
      - equal_to_cards_in_hand:
          id: 6
          label: "Equal to Cards in Hand"
          fields:
            - [owner, OwnerType]
      - equal_to_dice_roll:
          id: 8
          label: "Equal to Dice Roll"
          fields:
            - [count, i32]
            - [sides, i32]
      - equal_to_manapool:
          id: 9
          label: "Equal to Manapool"
          fields:
            - [color, ManaColor]
      - x_cost_spent:
          id: 10
          label: "X Cost Spent"
          fields:
            - [color, ManaColor]

- cascade:
    name: DurationType
    nodes:
      - permanent: { id: 1, label: "Permanent" }
      - turns:
          id: 2
          label: "Turns"
          fields:
            - [value, ValueType]
      - next_turn_phase:
          id: 3
          label: "Next Turn Phase"
          fields:
            - [phase, GamePhase]
      - end_of_phase:
          id: 4
          label: "End of Phase"
          fields:
            - [phase, GamePhase]
      - as_long_as_equipped:     { id: 5, label: "As Long as Equipped" }
      - as_long_as_card_in_play: { id: 6, label: "As Long as Card in Play" }

# Compact forms would be possible later, but only as syntactic sugar over this
# explicit form. The explicit form is the semantic truth.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 4) Value level
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# A `cascade<X>` value is NOT a loose map object, but a real value.
# Mental model:
#
#   ValueType.fixed(value=3)
#   DurationType.turns(value=ValueType.fixed(value=2))
#
# Example models:

- model:
    name: ActiveAbility
    fields:
      - [trigger, TriggerCode]
      - [effects, list<EffectCode>]
      - [costs,   list<ActivationCost>]

- model:
    name: Card
    fields:
      - [name, string]
      - [abilities, list<ActiveAbility>]

# Important point of my proposal:
#
# A `cascade` is used like a type of its own.
# So `TriggerCode`, `EffectCode`, `ActivationCost`, `ValueType`,
# `DurationType` are NOT enums plus side tables here, but directly the
# variant types themselves.
#
# The notation `cascade<X>` is thus only needed when talking about the
# meta type. In a field the concrete cascade type name is enough.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 5) Defaulting
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The most critical point for consistency is default creation.
#
# My proposal:
#
#   D1. `new SomeCascadeType` is only allowed if the type declares a default node
#       OR all callers specify a constructor with a node.
#
#   D2. Without a default node there is no silent "first element wins".
#
#   D3. If a default node exists, its fields must also be defaultable.
#
#
# Example:

- cascade:
    name: TriggerCode
    default: on_phase_start
    nodes:
      - on_phase_start:
          id: 1
          fields:
            - [phase, GamePhase, start]
      - on_phase_end:
          id: 2
          fields:
            - [phase, GamePhase, end]
      - on_effect_code:
          id: 3
          fields:
            - [effect, EffectCode]

# That way `new TriggerCode` is well-defined.
# Without `default:` only something like this would be allowed:
#   - decl: [trg, TriggerCode, { new: [TriggerCode.on_phase_start] }]
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 6) Node change
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# A node change is not a partial mutation of old fields, but a replacement
# of the complete variant state.
#
# Example:
#   Before: ValueType.equal_to_attribute(attribute=atk)
#   Change to: ValueType.fixed(value=1)
#
# Afterwards `attribute` no longer exists. There is only `value`.
#
# That prevents stale payloads and makes UI, validation and serialisation
# predictable.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 7) Serialisation
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# `cascade` must have a stable data format.
# My proposal:
#
#   serialize(cascade<X>) = [node_id, payload_field_0, payload_field_1, ...]
#
# Where:
#   - node_id comes from the declaration, never from auto-generated position indices
#   - the field order is exactly the declared `fields:` order
#   - recursive cascades keep serialising by the same principle
#
# This is deliberately simple. The language structure and the wire format
# coincide, as long as IDs are explicit and stable.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 8) Prefab binding as a consequence, not as the core
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Only when `cascade` is consistent as a type may the prefab runtime
# benefit from it.
#
# Minimal runtime contract for a widget `type: cascade`:
#   - root dropdown for node selection
#   - exactly one child widget per active payload field
#   - recursively for nested cascades
#   - widget state is only a view onto the model, not a second truth
#
# Example:

- prefab:
    name: AbilityRowPrefab
    binds: ActiveAbility
    width: 900
    height: auto
    children:
      - { name: trigger_lbl, type: label, x: 0,  y: 0,  label: "Trigger" }
      - { name: trigger,     type: cascade, x: 90, y: 0, bind: trigger }
      - { name: effects_lbl, type: label,   x: 0,  y: 40, label: "Effects" }
      - { name: effects,     type: list,    x: 90, y: 40, bind: effects, spawn: CascadeRowPrefab }
      - { name: costs_lbl,   type: label,   x: 0,  y: 220, label: "Costs" }
      - { name: costs,       type: list,    x: 90, y: 220, bind: costs, spawn: CascadeRowPrefab }

- prefab:
    name: CascadeRowPrefab
    binds: any
    width: 900
    height: auto
    children:
      - { name: value, type: cascade, x: 0, y: 0, bind: . }

# Important conceptual limit:
# This file does NOT define how ListPush, ListPop, OnClick, reflection or
# editor actions are implemented in detail. Those are follow-up features.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 9) What I deliberately do NOT take into the core
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# For me the following things do not belong in the basic definition of
# `cascade<T>`:
#
#   - generic button handlers
#   - stdlib ListPush/ListPop
#   - automatic method-ref wrapping rules
#   - localisation details
#   - complex runtime reflection APIs
#   - derived label heuristics as a semantic obligation
#
# All of these can make sense. But if the cascade core depends on them,
# the feature becomes needlessly broad and hard to keep consistent.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 10) Short version of my position
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# My consistent proposal is:
#
#   1. `cascade` is a real variant type.
#   2. A cascade value has exactly one active node.
#   3. The payload of other nodes does not continue to exist at the same time.
#   4. "Empty" is not implicit; optionality must be modelled explicitly.
#   5. IDs must be explicit and stable for wire-relevant types.
#   6. Prefab and editor behaviour is a direct projection of this type.
#
# If we keep these six points, `cascade<T>` becomes considerably more consistent
# for the card editor, for serialisation and for model-bound prefabs
# than flat slot models like `p0..p3`.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
